00:01:55.846 --> 00:01:57.733
So listen, without any further ado

00:01:57.733 --> 00:01:58.566
let's talk about what happened today.

00:01:58.566 --> 00:02:01.260
Let's talk about what was
announced, the Fed, the FOMC

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.390
the Federal Open Markets
Committee announcing

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:07.200
a 25 basis point increase
in the Fed funds rate

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.700
and the effective Fed funds rate.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:09.900
Wanna throw this chart up here

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:11.556
on your screen just to
give you some context

00:02:11.556 --> 00:02:13.050
of what we're looking at.

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:14.730
So where you see

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.892
at the right of your
screen, that's where we are.

00:02:16.892 --> 00:02:18.270
That line that basically
goes just straight up.

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:20.959
That's what the Fed has
done since March, 2022.

00:02:20.959 --> 00:02:23.520
Raising the Fed funds rate sometimes

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:26.024
by 75 basis points, 50 basis points

00:02:26.024 --> 00:02:29.850
and today another 25 basis
point increased them.

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:32.334
So they're slowing down
the rate of increases.

00:02:32.334 --> 00:02:35.190
And we are still now, but
if you look back there

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:37.980
we're at the highest level
that we've been since 2008.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:40.200
2008 were when weight started coming

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.509
down as a result of the
global financial crisis.

00:02:42.509 --> 00:02:45.116
The early days, things
like Lehman Brothers

00:02:45.116 --> 00:02:47.640
Bear Stearns had failed

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:49.440
and so you were seeing the Fed start say,

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.761
oh we need to start lowering rates.

00:02:51.761 --> 00:02:53.104
Well now we're getting them back

00:02:53.104 --> 00:02:55.020
up to that level again to try

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:58.259
and fight the inflation that
we have seen in the economy.

00:02:58.259 --> 00:03:00.930
Also important to note, as you see there

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:04.350
where we are is unusual rates
had been lower near zero.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:07.590
As you see rates rolling
along that zero lower bound

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:10.199
as it's called for most of this century,

00:03:10.199 --> 00:03:13.680
we are going to start, if
the Fed actually raises rates

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.780
to what the level that
they say they plan to

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:17.040
we're gonna get up to the highest level.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:20.100
We've been since back around 2000, 2001

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.556
when we hit that 2001 recession.

00:03:22.556 --> 00:03:24.927
It's interesting that
rates tend to get higher

00:03:24.927 --> 00:03:26.550
around these recession levels.

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:28.230
And then down when you get lower below

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:30.175
that that's when things
start to get better.

00:03:30.175 --> 00:03:31.620
We now could be getting

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:35.400
into some of those economic
worries that folks have had.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:36.810
So just wanted to put that up there

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:40.080
to illustrate where we are
right now and how that compares

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:41.491
to the environment that we had seen

00:03:41.491 --> 00:03:43.530
over the course of the last 20 years.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:45.630
This is a very unusual environment

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:46.812
and when you look at that Fed,

00:03:46.812 --> 00:03:49.080
that effective Fed funds rate chart

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:51.030
you can see just how unusual it is.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:52.530
So another thing I want to talk

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:54.750
about is the FOMC, the statement today

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:57.780
because the decision to raise
rates by 25 basis points

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:00.570
was accompanied by a statement
and it was also accompanied

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:02.940
by a press conference from the chairman.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:05.340
Really interesting changes
made to the statement

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.800
the Fed usually doesn't like
to change things too much.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:09.900
They change the date,
they change who voted.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:12.450
Shout out to our departed
colleague, Lael Brainard

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:15.367
the vice chair of the
Fed now moved on to work

00:04:15.367 --> 00:04:17.460
in the government under
President Joe Biden

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:20.007
but she was certainly a
very influential person,

00:04:20.007 --> 00:04:21.582
no longer with the Fed now,

00:04:21.582 --> 00:04:24.390
working with President
Biden and the government.

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:25.710
But we salute Fed Chair,

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:27.830
our former vice chair Lael Brainard.

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:29.820
Anyway, wanna talk about the statement

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:33.185
though very interesting
pieces because the Fed took

00:04:33.185 --> 00:04:36.960
out a statement saying,
job gains have been robust.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.420
They say instead picked
up in recent months

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:40.800
saying meaning essentially

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.363
that job growth is even more
than robust it's picked up,

00:04:43.363 --> 00:04:46.373
they took out a the reference to inflation

00:04:46.373 --> 00:04:48.240
has eased somewhat

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:50.594
and just now it says
inflation remains elevated.

00:04:50.594 --> 00:04:52.256
This is the Fed really saying

00:04:52.256 --> 00:04:55.440
jobs picking up inflation remains elevated

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:57.420
saying they're more
worried about inflation.

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:59.690
They continue to worry about
the growth of the economy.

00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:01.896
That's not the dovish rate hike

00:05:01.896 --> 00:05:03.990
that a lot of people
have been talking about.

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:05.070
I think that's why you're seeing some

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:06.300
of what you're seeing in markets.

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:07.133
We will get into that.

00:05:07.133 --> 00:05:07.966
Don't worry.

00:05:07.966 --> 00:05:11.160
Also, all the references to
Russia's war against Ukraine

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:11.993
which had been

00:05:11.993 --> 00:05:14.155
in the second paragraph of
the statement, removed cutout.

00:05:14.155 --> 00:05:16.980
Now it starts with the US banking system

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:19.140
is sound and resilient first sentence

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:20.460
in the second paragraph.

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:21.900
Recent developments are likely to result

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:24.720
in tighter credit conditions
for households and businesses

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:27.665
and to weigh on economic
activity, hiring and inflation.

00:05:27.665 --> 00:05:30.498
The extent of these effects is uncertain.

00:05:30.498 --> 00:05:34.140
The committee remains highly
attentive to inflation risks.

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:35.550
What that tells you is the committee

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:37.770
is now saying all right,
we're not worried as much

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:40.200
about Russia, Ukraine, all that inflation.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:41.858
Now we're worried about
this banking system

00:05:41.858 --> 00:05:43.950
which could urge us to low rates.

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:45.750
I think that may be why as soon

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:46.950
as the statement was released

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:48.630
you saw the stock market jump up

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:50.100
you saw stocks jump higher,

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:53.730
and then as the folks
were reading through this

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:55.744
the algorithms really
reacted immediately to that.

00:05:55.744 --> 00:05:58.200
But as real traders, as humans came back

00:05:58.200 --> 00:05:59.760
to their computers and
started making moves

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:02.190
you saw a reaction to the
rest of the statement.

00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:04.950
Those were the big important
changes in the statement.

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:06.810
So definitely keep your eye on that.

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:08.430
This banking system thing is something

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:09.990
the Fed is keeping watch on,

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:11.370
but that's the second paragraph.

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:13.401
Remember the first
paragraph, the Fed talks

00:06:13.401 --> 00:06:16.176
about inflation as job
growth has picked up

00:06:16.176 --> 00:06:19.980
and inflation remains
elevated, not has he somewhat.

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:20.970
We're taking that out.

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:22.500
Inflation remains elevated.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:24.360
That's big and that's a big piece

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:26.220
of why I think the market's
doing what it's doing.

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:27.147
Let's also talk about

00:06:27.147 --> 00:06:31.230
how the Fed's balance sheet has changed

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:32.550
and what's been happening

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:34.860
because as a result of this banking issue

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:36.960
I don't wanna call it a
banking crisis or a bank run

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:38.010
but as a result of some

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:40.800
of these issues with the
banks, the Fed has made moves.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:42.510
So on the one hand they're saying, okay

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:43.800
we're watching this.

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:45.600
On the other hand, I wanna
throw something up here

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:48.570
on your screen just to show
you some of the actions

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:49.590
that the Fed has been taking

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:51.115
because a lot of this is about words,

00:06:51.115 --> 00:06:52.461
but it's also about actions.

00:06:52.461 --> 00:06:55.050
And so just want to put this chart up here

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:57.420
at the Fed's balance sheet on your screen.

00:06:57.420 --> 00:06:59.970
Now what you see is
just that parabolic rise

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:02.910
from 2020 Fed's balance sheet
just goes up and up and up.

00:07:02.910 --> 00:07:03.908
That's what we, they called QE

00:07:03.908 --> 00:07:07.290
or what I guess we in the
media called QE Infinity

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:09.814
where the Fed said we will buy
as many bonds as necessary.

00:07:09.814 --> 00:07:12.000
They ratcheted that
balance sheet up and up

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:14.960
and up and up to levels that
had never reached before

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:16.957
had never even been close to before,

00:07:16.957 --> 00:07:18.783
we're always in unprecedented territory.

00:07:18.783 --> 00:07:21.644
And you got to the balance
sheet being about $9 trillion

00:07:21.644 --> 00:07:24.552
as in the Fed had bought $9 trillion worth

00:07:24.552 --> 00:07:28.740
of bonds and other things,
some of 'em being bonds

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:31.500
from junk rated companies,
some being big companies

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:33.690
like Apple, Verizon, things like that.

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:35.220
The Fed was basically
buying anything could get

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:37.470
its hands on and you saw
that rise all the way

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:39.930
up to about $9 trillion on
the Fed's balance sheet.

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:41.460
That was money they were pumping

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:43.710
out in the economy by buying these bonds.

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:45.420
And then you see that number that kind of,

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:46.920
it starts to come down there

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:48.840
and then at the very
right edge of your screen

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:51.120
you see that big tick up,
that just straight line.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:53.730
That was last week and that's what the Fed

00:07:53.730 --> 00:07:55.230
did in response to this banking issue.

00:07:55.230 --> 00:07:57.522
That's almost $300 billion.

00:07:57.522 --> 00:08:00.240
The Fed added to its balance
sheet and that is huge.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:01.800
Now, that's more than the week

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:04.479
after Lehman Brothers
collapsed in October, 2008.

00:08:04.479 --> 00:08:06.300
Even when accounting for inflation.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:07.920
So even when you factor in inflation,

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:10.140
the Fed did more last week

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:12.202
than they did after
Lehman Brothers collapsed.

00:08:12.202 --> 00:08:14.340
Even though we're talking

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:18.337
about what they're saying is a
far less worrisome situation.

00:08:18.337 --> 00:08:20.160
So keep that in mind now.

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:21.060
I just wanted to throw that up there

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:23.910
to illustrate just how
big and how substantial

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:26.836
the Fed's response to that banking crisis.

00:08:26.836 --> 00:08:28.645
The issues with Silicon Valley Bank,

00:08:28.645 --> 00:08:30.600
Signature Bank and others.

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:32.430
This worries about First Republic

00:08:32.430 --> 00:08:35.070
and all those things had been,
the Fed was clearly worried.

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:36.749
They injected a lot of
money into the system.

00:08:36.749 --> 00:08:38.280
That's a lot of money that goes

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:40.830
out into the economy,
at least hypothetically

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:42.900
when the Fed's doing all
of these things adding

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:45.270
to its balance sheet,
they say, we're doing this

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:47.520
because we're trying to
support and uplift the economy.

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:50.070
Well, as they're trying
to reign in the economy

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:52.029
raise rates to kind of lower inflation

00:08:52.029 --> 00:08:54.544
they also just pumped about $300 billion

00:08:54.544 --> 00:08:56.478
which is kind of doing the opposite

00:08:56.478 --> 00:08:58.140
of what they had talked about.

00:08:58.140 --> 00:08:59.850
Now, Fed chair, Jerome Powell was asked

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:01.020
about this during the press conference.

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:02.850
He kinda said, oh, this is different.

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:04.403
But listen, they're buying bonds

00:09:04.403 --> 00:09:06.390
they're pumping money into the economy.

00:09:06.390 --> 00:09:08.310
They're pushing money
into the banking system

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:09.865
which is exactly what
they said they were doing

00:09:09.865 --> 00:09:12.600
to help offset some of the issues

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:15.960
from the economic crisis at 2008, in 2020

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:17.580
and in the years that followed that

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:19.920
when you see that balance
sheet just go up and up and up.

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:21.930
So this is important to watch out for.

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:24.180
Now, let's talk about the Fed's summary

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:27.540
of economic projections or
how the Fed sees the economy.

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:30.060
What I want to throw up here
is the so-called dot plot.

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:31.229
These are FOMC members

00:09:31.229 --> 00:09:33.600
quote unquote assessments of the economy.

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:35.340
And just wanna put this up on your screen.

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:39.630
This is the December
dot plot of FOMC members

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:42.630
and as you can see there,
it's right around 5.1%.

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:44.550
There wasn't a big change from December

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:46.647
which is about three months ago

00:09:46.647 --> 00:09:48.270
their last meeting when they
did the dot plot to today.

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:51.270
But you see numbers
hovering around for 2023

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:53.520
expecting rates to be about 5.1%

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:56.370
a few lower, a more than a few higher.

00:09:56.370 --> 00:10:00.750
But all in all, the expectation
is 5.1 points, 5.1%.

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:01.583
Now let's throw

00:10:01.583 --> 00:10:03.420
up the March summary of
economic projections.

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:05.220
The dot plot, this is March now,

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:08.070
you see it's very similar
but a little different

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:10.950
in that you see there are
a couple dots moved higher,

00:10:10.950 --> 00:10:12.390
no one's moved lower.

00:10:12.390 --> 00:10:14.730
There's in fact less dots you see there

00:10:14.730 --> 00:10:18.150
under that 5% level than
there were in December, right?

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:19.980
There's just that one dot under 5%.

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:21.765
Everyone expects rates to be at least 5%.

00:10:21.765 --> 00:10:24.150
A couple people have
raised their expectations

00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:27.891
above five and a half percent
and now there's even one,

00:10:27.891 --> 00:10:29.010
there's that one more dot even higher

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:30.510
almost at that 6% level.

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:32.880
So wanna put that up just to illustrate

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:35.130
that this was not a
hike where the Fed said,

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:36.390
okay we're hiking 'cause we have to

00:10:36.390 --> 00:10:38.670
but we think really
things are gonna go easy

00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:40.860
and smooth as we move
forward in the future.

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:41.940
No, no, no.

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:44.400
The Fed is saying we still
think we have to do more

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:45.420
and that has a lot to do

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:48.090
with the data that we've
seen and that has a lot to do

00:10:48.090 --> 00:10:50.730
with the environment we saw
coming into this report.

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:52.230
So let's talk about that.

00:10:52.230 --> 00:10:55.440
The environment we saw
coming into today's meeting

00:10:55.440 --> 00:11:00.240
and to today's decision to
raise rates by 25 basis points

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:01.650
'cause we know why they did that.

00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:03.930
First of all, CPI and PPI

00:11:03.930 --> 00:11:06.570
that's a consumer price index
and a producer price index.

00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:08.370
What individuals you and I are paying

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:09.270
when we go to the store

00:11:09.270 --> 00:11:11.490
and what businesses are
paying for their goods.

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:14.760
Those have been coming
down consistently declining

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:17.190
for about the past six,
seven, eight months now.

00:11:17.190 --> 00:11:18.450
We've seen those decline

00:11:18.450 --> 00:11:21.510
and we've seen also the
outsized role of shelter

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:25.260
AKA housing, aka rent
prices and home prices.

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:27.000
That's been holding it up even though

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:29.130
the fed's real-time indicators have shown

00:11:29.130 --> 00:11:30.150
that that's declining.

00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:31.080
Fed Chair Powell mentioned

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:32.100
that today in the press conference.

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:34.463
He says, we know that new
leases are being signed

00:11:34.463 --> 00:11:36.360
at the pace of inflation

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:38.820
in leases and in rent is coming down.

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:40.710
We've seen that even though

00:11:40.710 --> 00:11:43.627
in CPI and in PPI, it's
keeping, it's staying elevated.

00:11:43.627 --> 00:11:46.440
Remember if you watch Market Takes

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:49.468
when we did CPI at our last

00:11:49.468 --> 00:11:52.578
this was last week on I
believe the 15th of March.

00:11:52.578 --> 00:11:54.638
We talked about the outsized role

00:11:54.638 --> 00:11:57.661
that shelter AKA housing was playing.

00:11:57.661 --> 00:12:00.600
And we know that this
is a lagging indicator.

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:01.433
It takes a long time

00:12:01.433 --> 00:12:03.105
for this to get factored into the reports.

00:12:03.105 --> 00:12:06.670
And so when you're seeing
shelter holding up CPI,

00:12:06.670 --> 00:12:09.069
well you can assume, and
it's probably safe to assume

00:12:09.069 --> 00:12:10.707
that CPI is actually lower

00:12:10.707 --> 00:12:12.960
than what we're seeing
in some of those numbers

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:15.030
and PPI is lower than what we're seeing

00:12:15.030 --> 00:12:16.913
even though it plays a much smaller part.

00:12:16.913 --> 00:12:19.145
So with CPI being lower in inflation

00:12:19.145 --> 00:12:22.243
for consumers overall being
lower, that's good news for Fed.

00:12:22.243 --> 00:12:23.587
It says that they can slow down.

00:12:23.587 --> 00:12:26.744
We also have seen that over the course

00:12:26.744 --> 00:12:30.660
of this time services
inflation has been picking

00:12:30.660 --> 00:12:31.584
up while goods inflation

00:12:31.584 --> 00:12:33.780
which drove a big part of the increase

00:12:33.780 --> 00:12:36.546
in inflation over the
past couple years or so.

00:12:36.546 --> 00:12:39.180
As we've been in this pandemic environment

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:40.600
or post pandemic environment.

00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:42.750
Goods prices have been picking up, well,

00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:44.100
they've come down considerably,

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:46.397
but now services prices are coming up

00:12:46.397 --> 00:12:48.420
as in what you pay when
you go to the doctor

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:49.500
what you're paying when you're going out

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:52.850
to the bar restaurants,
when you go see a specialist

00:12:52.850 --> 00:12:55.560
for some medicine or when you
just go to the amusement park.

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:57.184
All those things are
services rather than goods.

00:12:57.184 --> 00:12:59.430
Those are things that you buy on Amazon

00:12:59.430 --> 00:13:02.160
or when you go to the store,
those prices are rising

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:03.960
even while goods prices are declining.

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:05.700
And that's what the
Fed is concerned about.

00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:08.153
Fed chair, Jerome Powell
spotlighted that today.

00:13:08.153 --> 00:13:10.920
One thing to worry about

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:13.000
with that as well as
been the jobs reports,

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:15.748
jobs have been growing at a very fast pace

00:13:15.748 --> 00:13:18.995
and we have not seen the
sort of moderation or decline

00:13:18.995 --> 00:13:21.810
in pickup and jobs growth
that the Fed wants to see.

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:23.040
The Fed wants to see.

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:25.110
It is said in its summary
of economic projections

00:13:25.110 --> 00:13:27.540
it expects unemployment to rise about 1%.

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:29.430
That means millions of
people lose their jobs.

00:13:29.430 --> 00:13:31.620
They also wanna see fewer job openings.

00:13:31.620 --> 00:13:33.480
And what we saw in the JOLTS report,

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:35.640
the Job Opening and Labor Turnover Survey

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:39.120
was there have been
fewer job openings added

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:41.220
as in people are posting
fewer jobs looking

00:13:41.220 --> 00:13:44.460
for employees than they had previously.

00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:46.350
That has come down a
little bit but not a lot

00:13:46.350 --> 00:13:48.480
and we're still seeing
that at a very high rate.

00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:51.480
So all those things support
kind of the Fed moving

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:53.190
taking its foot off the brake

00:13:53.190 --> 00:13:55.440
and sort of slowing down
doing a little bit less.

00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:58.830
On the other side of that, we
do have the China reopening.

00:13:58.830 --> 00:14:03.830
We have that services increase
in prices that we've seen

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:06.000
and that affects a lot more of the economy

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:09.062
because the US is a mostly
service-based economies economy

00:14:09.062 --> 00:14:12.000
excuse me, unlike other
places like Germany, China

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:14.476
et cetera, that are still
based on exporting goods.

00:14:14.476 --> 00:14:18.390
You've got also with China
reopening, you've got a number

00:14:18.390 --> 00:14:20.280
of other countries
seeing inflation pick up.

00:14:20.280 --> 00:14:22.440
France inflation or
inflation in France rose

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:24.979
to 6.2% in February up from January.

00:14:24.979 --> 00:14:29.160
Inflation in Japan rose to
4.4% up from in January.

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:31.470
In the UK, inflation
is still double digits

00:14:31.470 --> 00:14:33.840
and the euros on inflation
fell just a little bit

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:35.069
from 8.6 to 8.5%.

00:14:35.069 --> 00:14:36.420
What's that telling us?

00:14:36.420 --> 00:14:39.420
That saying that all over
the world, inflation is still

00:14:39.420 --> 00:14:42.090
either picking up or coming
down just a little bit

00:14:42.090 --> 00:14:44.290
and that impacts us because
this is a global economy.

00:14:44.290 --> 00:14:47.190
No economy exists in a vacuum.

00:14:47.190 --> 00:14:50.430
Everything happens with
relation to other economies.

00:14:50.430 --> 00:14:51.593
All those things are worrisome.

00:14:51.593 --> 00:14:53.757
Also, we saw those existing home sales,

00:14:53.757 --> 00:14:55.463
the report that came out earlier this week

00:14:55.463 --> 00:14:58.115
showed existing home sales up 14.5%.

00:14:58.115 --> 00:15:00.390
That's the thing the fed's policy

00:15:00.390 --> 00:15:02.880
can have the most direct
impact on and it's showing

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:04.620
that existing home sales spiked up

00:15:04.620 --> 00:15:06.537
after coming down consistently
for a number of months.

00:15:06.537 --> 00:15:09.216
Housing starts also up by about 10%.

00:15:09.216 --> 00:15:11.552
This shows the Fed is not slowing demand.

00:15:11.552 --> 00:15:13.560
That's gotta be something
they're worried about

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:15.810
and that could be why we saw

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:20.160
what I think is a hawkish rate
hike this month from the Fed.

00:15:20.160 --> 00:15:21.180
So keep that in mind.

00:15:21.180 --> 00:15:22.787
On the other side of that also, of course,

00:15:22.787 --> 00:15:24.900
oil and gas prices have been coming lower,

00:15:24.900 --> 00:15:27.198
natural gas has been just
jumping off a cliff price wise.

00:15:27.198 --> 00:15:28.624
And that means

00:15:28.624 --> 00:15:31.631
that you're seeing different
forces kind of converging

00:15:31.631 --> 00:15:34.347
and we're gonna see which one breaks out.

00:15:34.347 --> 00:15:37.740
Now, inflation has
consistently moved lower

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:39.690
in recent months, but we've also seen

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:41.558
this banking crisis that could be evidence

00:15:41.558 --> 00:15:42.916
that things are breaking

00:15:42.916 --> 00:15:45.450
within the economy and the
Fed needs to slow down.

00:15:45.450 --> 00:15:48.090
I'm seeing a lot of folks,
economists, market watchers

00:15:48.090 --> 00:15:49.311
et cetera in my inbox saying,

00:15:49.311 --> 00:15:51.630
"Dion, the Fed is making a mistake.

00:15:51.630 --> 00:15:53.700
They need to slow down, they
need to stop raising rates

00:15:53.700 --> 00:15:55.740
they need to reevaluate
what they're doing."

00:15:55.740 --> 00:15:57.390
That's all because, and not all

00:15:57.390 --> 00:15:59.913
because actually I should say
that case has been increased

00:15:59.913 --> 00:16:02.130
or you know folks have pointed

00:16:02.130 --> 00:16:03.690
to this and said this is evidence

00:16:03.690 --> 00:16:05.220
that the Fed needs to slow down and stop

00:16:05.220 --> 00:16:08.010
because the problems in banking
are gonna hurt businesses

00:16:08.010 --> 00:16:11.730
especially small and mid-size
businesses that rely on banks.

00:16:11.730 --> 00:16:12.960
These big companies, they go out

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:14.850
in the markets and they
can borrow money there

00:16:14.850 --> 00:16:17.266
but these small and medium
businesses that need banks,

00:16:17.266 --> 00:16:19.170
banks are gonna pull back because of this.

00:16:19.170 --> 00:16:20.310
There's a lot of worry

00:16:20.310 --> 00:16:23.280
about what banks are doing
and not going out and lending

00:16:23.280 --> 00:16:24.540
and that's gonna hurt businesses,

00:16:24.540 --> 00:16:25.800
that's gonna hurt mom and pop

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:28.151
and that's gonna hit people
right in their pocketbooks.

00:16:28.151 --> 00:16:31.263
Really quickly, wanna check
in on the markets real fast?

00:16:31.263 --> 00:16:34.500
We've got the Dow and the
S and P and the Nasdaq

00:16:34.500 --> 00:16:37.063
all down significantly down about 1.6%.

00:16:37.063 --> 00:16:40.320
Each index is hovering
right around that level.

00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:43.080
We saw a huge nose dive in stock prices

00:16:43.080 --> 00:16:45.039
and I think that's because
of this hawkishness

00:16:45.039 --> 00:16:47.190
that we're seeing from the Fed.

00:16:47.190 --> 00:16:48.600
Fed saying they can basically

00:16:48.600 --> 00:16:50.811
they're not prepared to back
off what they were doing.

00:16:50.811 --> 00:16:52.557
The market could be
thinking one of two things.

00:16:52.557 --> 00:16:53.718
One could be

00:16:53.718 --> 00:16:56.280
that the Fed isn't seeing
the force for the trees

00:16:56.280 --> 00:16:58.920
that they're not understanding
what's happening here

00:16:58.920 --> 00:17:00.990
in the market and that
they're making a huge mistake.

00:17:00.990 --> 00:17:02.917
The other possibility
is that they see, okay

00:17:02.917 --> 00:17:04.907
the Fed is determined
to get inflation down.

00:17:04.907 --> 00:17:08.520
And so we are seeing
these moves and inflation

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:12.427
the ones we talked about, price
is moving higher on services

00:17:12.427 --> 00:17:15.900
things like job increases continuing

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:18.954
and they've seen, okay
the Fed is gonna get back

00:17:18.954 --> 00:17:20.430
to doing what it was doing
when we were worried.

00:17:20.430 --> 00:17:23.130
Now it's time to sell stocks,
it's time to get outta risk,

00:17:23.130 --> 00:17:24.930
it's time to move things lower

00:17:24.930 --> 00:17:26.430
because the Fed is gonna be back

00:17:26.430 --> 00:17:28.740
on its horse making moves, taking action.

00:17:28.740 --> 00:17:30.990
And that's not gonna be good for stocks.

00:17:30.990 --> 00:17:32.310
It could be either one of those.

00:17:32.310 --> 00:17:34.350
We're seeing the same
move in the bond market.

00:17:34.350 --> 00:17:36.240
You're seeing bond prices move higher

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:37.800
which means those yields
are falling lower.

00:17:37.800 --> 00:17:39.320
Really across the board,

00:17:39.320 --> 00:17:42.720
the six month is up about
five basis points today

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:45.903
in price, down about five basis
points, excuse me, in yield.

00:17:45.903 --> 00:17:49.496
The 10 year is down about
14 basis points in yield.

00:17:49.496 --> 00:17:52.080
These are some pretty
significant moves five year

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:52.950
in the three year down

00:17:52.950 --> 00:17:56.714
about 23 basis points in
terms of the yield there.

00:17:56.714 --> 00:17:59.850
Also, let's check out crude futures,

00:17:59.850 --> 00:18:00.683
I think is really interesting.

00:18:00.683 --> 00:18:02.570
Brent crude is down about 1%.

00:18:02.570 --> 00:18:04.830
WTI is up about half percent.

00:18:04.830 --> 00:18:07.331
Very interesting, but gold
and silver are up huge.

00:18:07.331 --> 00:18:10.650
That shows you that the market
is worried about recession.

00:18:10.650 --> 00:18:14.820
We're seeing gold up
about 1.8%, almost 2%,

00:18:14.820 --> 00:18:16.123
silver up almost 3%.

00:18:16.123 --> 00:18:18.547
Those are generally
worries about recession

00:18:18.547 --> 00:18:21.660
